{"kind":"report","text":"approved at exact head 20b3e3f97fc04abe76ba62233705bed7aae0044a. I verified the page's behaviour claims against the source at this head rather than reading it for plausibility, and every claim I checked held.\n\nARTIFACT SET, your first condition. All six are effective, live, not retired, not stale and not world-stale: docs 365c04ca at 20b3e3f9, and the five frontmatter behaviour bases — cmd/gitseq-github 9f09f57d at d4f54392, cmd/gs 5c8248f2 at 3c312552, internal/connector 8a446fa7 at 6fad9e8a, internal/residentclient 4288e38f at 889a64a2, internal/app bcb5bb1d at 3c312552. I checked each one individually rather than accepting the set. The head is clean, pushed to origin/request/connect-github-howto, cut from current main 6fad9e8a, and the change is docs-only: two files, +270 lines, no source touched.\n\nCLAIMS VERIFIED AGAINST SOURCE, with where I read them:\n\n- The observe/propose authority asymmetry is real and is the best thing in this change. charterAuthorizes (cmd/gitseq-github/main.go:417-430) does exactly what the page says: a charter with no `operations` field returns nil for observe and an error for propose, and a charter that declares operations authorizes only those named. The page's sentence \"observation authority never silently grants authority to open a pull request\" is not aspirational; it is enforced, and the code comment gives the same reason the page does — reading is recoverable by supersession, a pull request opened under the project's name is not.\n- Bounds are exact, not approximate: maxIssuePages = 100, maxIssueRecords = 10_000, maxResponseBodyBytes = 8 \u003c\u003c 20 (fetch.go:24-30), with io.LimitReader applied at :238. The page's \"at most 100 pages or 10,000 records, and each decoded response is limited to 8 MiB\" matches all three.\n- Pull requests really are excluded from the issues API results (fetch.go:119 and :164 both skip on a non-nil PullRequest field).\n- The observation body carries exactly the six documented fields and no more (observe.go:92-97 plus AdmittedBy at :74). The issue body is genuinely not copied — Issue.Body exists on the fetched struct and is deliberately absent from the Observation, with the comment saying anything the connector did not see has no business in the body.\n- The idempotency key is the external identifier and nothing else (observe.go:88), which is what makes the page's \"a repeated pass does not append a second observation\" true rather than hopeful.\n- The token is read once from the process environment (main.go:74) and sent as a bearer header (fetch.go:202, :311). Nothing writes it to disk or refreshes it, so \"does not read a token file, refresh a token, or store the token\" is accurate.\n- The propose warning is accurate and is the most valuable paragraph on the page. proposePullRequest (main.go:209-220) requires all six flags non-empty, and proposalIsCoherent checks the request and artifact are effective and live, that the artifact names --commit, and that it rests on the request. It does NOT verify that --branch points at --commit, that the issue number is positive or was observed, or that the branch and base exist. So \"Check the Git state yourself before removing --dry-run\" describes a real gap before a non-idempotent write, and the page is right to put it in bold.\n- --server having no effect on --propose is structural: proposePullRequest takes only the GitHub client and no workroom writer.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Documentation only. No package boundary, exported surface or call order moves, so no layer contract in docs/reference/architecture.md changes and that page needs no update in this head. The layers the page describes are the connector (internal/connector/github), the connector command (cmd/gitseq-github), and the resident transport boundary (internal/residentclient) — and the frontmatter rests on artifacts for all three plus internal/app and cmd/gs, which is the right shape: this page describes behaviour, so it rests on the behaviour's artifacts and will flare when they move. That is discipline 8 done properly rather than resting only on the request.\n\nSECURITY. This page tells a reader how to wire an external forge into a signed log, so I gave it the most attention, and it is honest where honesty is uncomfortable.\n\nThe authority boundaries are stated correctly and enforced: charter must be present, ratified, live and non-stale; clauses must be operator-signed and effective, with the code passing the fold's decision rather than mere presence in Statements — the comment at main.go:432-439 records that as a bug the author made twice before naming it. The GitHub login is carried as data and never as a roster identity, and the page says so. Credentials are correctly described as unmanaged and unnarrowed, and the page states plainly that the token's GitHub permissions may exceed the charter's workroom scope and that the charter cannot reduce the token — that is the right warning and it is easy to omit.\n\nOne observation, NOT a blocker and not a reason to respin. Untrusted issue input does cross into the durable record: the issue title flows verbatim into both the assertion text and the body `title` field (observe.go:91-97) with no length bound or sanitisation at that layer. The 8 MiB response cap bounds it in practice and the framing is defensible — the connector attests that it observed content attributed to a login, which is exactly what the page says it does — so nothing here is wrong. But the page's security discussion covers the login-is-not-an-identity question without quite saying that the title is attacker-controlled text landing inside a signed act. A sentence to that effect would complete the picture for a reader deciding their admission clauses. Fold it in only if you are touching the file; do not respin for it.\n\nI also confirmed the page does not oversell: the \"Deliberate non-features and policy gaps\" section correctly names comment ingestion, webhooks, reconciliation and credential management as absent, and correctly labels proposals #2303, #2396 and #2483 as unratified discussion rather than policy. A guide that says what it cannot do is worth more than one that implies completeness.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Nothing to cut. At 269 lines it is long for a how-to, but the length is carrying warnings and exact field tables rather than prose, and I could not find a paragraph whose removal would not lose a fact a reader needs. The disposable mktemp example is the one candidate I considered and rejected: it exercises local custody setup without contacting GitHub, which is genuinely useful for someone verifying their actor provisioning before spending a token.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact tree with -count=1 rather than cited:\n- go test -count=1 . (root public-surface, including TestLocalMarkdownLinks): ok 0.255s — this covers the new docs/README.md link\n- go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/...: ok 79.099s\n- go test -count=1 ./internal/connector/... ./cmd/gitseq-github/: ok 0.429s and ok 3.460s\n- go vet ./internal/connector/... ./cmd/gitseq-github/: clean\nAll green. The worktree is clean at this head.\n\nRatify this verdict before merging. I note that a later merge still requires Hugh's separately ratified adoption proposal, and this verdict authorises nothing beyond the review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:365c04ca04fbc41cbcbaae8503fe9181d33ae702","head":"20b3e3f97fc04abe76ba62233705bed7aae0044a","verdict":"approved"}}